WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.560
If you spill coffee on your laptop today, you are going to hate yourself significantly more

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:08.840
than you would have just yesterday.

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:09.840
Oh, absolutely.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.000
I mean, Apple just quietly jacked up the prices on their budget laptop.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:20.720
The MacBook Neo went from $599 to $699 and the iPad Air.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.480
That's up a full $150 overnight.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:27.720
Which is wild because it completely shatters the golden rule of consumer tech, right?

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:28.720
Yeah.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:32.440
It's always been that your devices should get cheaper over time or at the very least

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:37.160
you get a significantly more powerful machine for the exact same price.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:38.160
Yeah.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.240
But we're watching that whole paradigm just reverse in real time.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:42.760
And it's not happening in a vacuum.

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:46.980
Today we're doing a deep dive into a stack of recent economic reports, a new Supreme

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:51.080
Court brief, and a flurry of fresh legal filings from the NCAA.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:52.480
A lot of ground to cover.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:53.480
Yeah.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:58.680
But our mission for this deep dive is to figure out why the basic rules of everyday life from

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:03.920
what you pay for a tablet to the warning labels on your garden supplies to the sports

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:08.280
you watch on a Saturday are just dramatically shifting beneath our feet.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:13.220
And the common denominator across all these sources is that our massive foundational institutions

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:17.120
are entirely lagging behind the reality they're supposed to be regulating.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:18.120
Right.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:21.720
And let's actually start with that sticker shock on the Apple products because this isn't

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:27.080
just, you know, a massive tech company deciding to arbitrarily pad its profit margins.

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:28.080
Okay.

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:33.880
Well, we're looking at a macro economic phenomenon that analysts are starting called Iflation.

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:38.560
It's this structural systemic shift in how technology is priced.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:43.400
And it all traces back directly to the Silicon Valley AI boom, I'm going to need you to connect

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:44.400
those dots for me.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:45.400
Sure.

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:49.640
Because from a consumer's perspective, I don't really get how a tech giant's obsession

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:55.360
with building a massive AI chatbot translates to like my kids, basic school laptop suddenly

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.800
costing 15% more at the local big box store.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:00.880
Those feel like two completely separate economies.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:05.480
They do feel separate, but they share the exact same underlying plumbing.

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:11.040
It basically comes down to this invisible tug of war happening deep inside the global semiconductor

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:12.560
supply chain.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:15.800
Specifically, we're talking about memory and storage chips.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:16.800
Okay.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:22.360
Right now, there is just an absolute unbridled gold rush for the highly specialized hardware

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:28.040
needed to power AI servers, Nvidia and its competitors are placing massive orders.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:31.480
So imagine you operate a semiconductor fabrication plant, a fab.

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:35.040
You mean one of those multi billion dollar factories with the hyper sterile queen rooms

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.920
where they slice up the silicon wafers nicely?

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.040
Those facilities are physically finite.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:41.840
You can't just build a new fab overnight.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:46.040
I mean, it takes years of construction and billions of dollars in capital.

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:51.320
Furthermore, a manufacturing line isn't a magical faucet you can just crank wider.

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:56.400
If you want to produce the complex high bandwidth memory chips that AI data centers require,

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:59.520
you actually have to retool your existing assembly lines.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:04.720
You have to physically transition your factory floor away from producing standard NAND flash

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:05.720
memory.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:09.480
And that standard memory is what goes into a consumer tablet or a budget laptop.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:10.480
Exactly.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:11.480
Okay.

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:15.440
So there is a literal physical bottleneck here.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:20.140
Every time a factory decides to chase the highly lucrative AI chip market, they are

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:23.700
actively choosing to produce fewer standard consumer chips.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:25.340
That's the mechanism driving all of this.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:30.780
The factories are overwhelmingly prioritizing AI chips because the margins are just astronomical.

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:34.500
As a direct result, the global supply of standard consumer device chips is suddenly

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:36.500
artificially constrained.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:40.940
And when supply drops that aggressively, but the baseline consumer demand for every electronic

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:43.700
stays the same, the suppliers hold all the cards.

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:45.260
They have massive pricing power.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:50.380
It sounds like the entire tech economy is essentially experiencing surge pricing, like

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:54.660
trying to get an Uber during a thunderstorm, except the thunderstorm is the AI boom.

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:57.740
Surge pricing is the perfect way to visualize it.

00:03:57.740 --> 00:04:01.860
And the latest market data validates this in a rather alarming way.

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:06.420
Look at Micron, a major supplier of memory chips for companies like Apple.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:09.740
They recently reported absolutely blockbuster earnings.

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:11.260
Let me guess because of the surge pricing.

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:12.260
Yep.

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:16.100
The underlying reason is that the cost of those specific memory chips has spiked more

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:19.060
than four-fold since the fourth quarter of last year.

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:21.660
Wait, four times the cost and less than a year?

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:22.660
Yeah.

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:23.660
That is staggering.

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:27.780
I mean, it makes sense why Apple would say they haven't seen component prices increase

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:29.580
this much this quickly.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:32.380
And it stretches far beyond Apple, too.

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:36.980
Government data indicates that overall electronics prices rose about 6% from December to May.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:41.140
Microsoft just had to raise the retail prices on their Xbox consoles for the exact same

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:45.020
reason the escalating costs of memory and storage components.

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:50.340
So we are essentially paying a hidden AI tax on our video games and our budget laptops.

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:51.340
We are.

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:56.580
And this is where the dynamic scales up from a frustrating consumer hardware issue to a

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:59.300
serious macroeconomic threat.

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:04.260
If we zoom out and look at the national economy, the Federal Reserve is monitoring this exact

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:07.980
supply chain squeeze with a massive amount of anxiety.

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:12.360
Which tracks considering the Fed's entire mandate over the last couple of years has

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:17.220
been trying to wrestle general inflation back down to Earth by just aggressively hiking

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:18.220
interest rates.

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:19.220
Right.

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:23.180
Federal Reserve Governor Lisa Cook actually recently issued a very pointed warning about

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:25.180
this specific friction.

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:29.580
She highlighted that AI companies are not slowing down their infrastructure build out.

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:32.980
They plan to keep spending billions upon billions on these data centers.

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:33.980
Okay.

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:37.380
So that means this inflation, the spiking prices for electronics and related tech products,

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:41.420
could become deeply entrenched, meaning the central bank might be forced to keep interest

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:46.220
rates elevated for much longer than anyone hoped, just to cool off a tech driven price

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:50.620
surge, or worst case scenario, they might even have to raise raise higher.

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:52.140
The implications are massive.

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:56.260
The cost of your mortgage, your auto loan or your credit card debt could stay painfully

00:05:56.260 --> 00:06:02.140
high simply because Silicon Valley is locked in a fierce arms race to build data centers.

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:05.060
Is there any silver lining here?

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:10.940
Because paying higher interest rates to fund someone else's server farm doesn't really

00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:13.420
feel like a win for the average worker.

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:16.060
There is an alternative perspective within the Fed.

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:20.180
Fed Chair Kevin Warsh holds a notably more optimistic view.

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:25.260
His thesis is that all of this massive AI investment will eventually trigger a historic boom in

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:26.260
worker productivity.

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:27.260
Okay.

00:06:27.260 --> 00:06:28.260
How so?

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:31.460
What the economic theory is that if AI makes the American workforce significantly faster

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:36.660
and more efficient, that widespread productivity will naturally offset the rising costs and

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:39.860
eventually tame inflation on a structural level.

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:42.060
The operative word there being eventually.

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:46.220
I mean, if we look at historical parallels like the dawn of the internet or even the roll

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:50.340
out of widespread electricity, a structural productivity boom of that magnitude takes

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:55.580
years, if not decades, to actually materialize and trickle down to the average paycheck.

00:06:55.580 --> 00:06:59.620
That is the massive caveat that most economists are flagging right now.

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:01.620
The timeline is misaligned.

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:06.500
So what this means for you and me today is that we are basically paying a premium at

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:11.900
the cash register for a technological utopia that might be a decade away.

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:17.140
Like we are funding the AI revolution through more expensive iPads and higher mortgages, but

00:07:17.140 --> 00:07:20.100
we aren't seeing the promised economic benefits yet.

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:21.100
Exactly.

00:07:21.100 --> 00:07:25.300
We are trapped in a highly awkward, very expensive transitional phase.

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:29.980
The Federal Reserve, which is an institution built for traditional economic cycles, is struggling

00:07:29.980 --> 00:07:34.660
to regulate an economy that is suddenly being warped by an artificial intelligence boom,

00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:38.660
which perfectly illustrates the theme emerging from our sources today.

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:44.380
But the Fed isn't the only foundational institution totally unprepared for how fast reality is moving.

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:49.180
If we pivot from the round of macroeconomics to the highest levels of the federal legal system,

00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:52.780
we see the exact same institutional lag happening with science.

00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:56.860
We see it clearly in the Supreme Court ruling that was handed down yesterday.

00:07:56.860 --> 00:08:03.780
In a 72 decision, the Supreme Court delivered a massive sweeping victory to Bayer, the multinational

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:05.980
pharmaceutical and biotechnology conglomerate.

00:08:05.980 --> 00:08:08.300
Let's get into the mechanics of this ruling.

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:09.860
What exactly was it stake here?

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:14.700
The ruling essentially shields Bayer from being sued in state level courts over claims

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:20.100
that it failed to warn customers that its flagship herbicide roundup causes cancer.

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:22.860
The contradiction at the heart of this case is fascinating.

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:28.100
On one side, you have the World Health Organization explicitly stating that roundups key active

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:33.500
ingredient a chemical called glyphosate is, quote, probably carcinogenic.

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:37.540
But then on the other side, you have the US Environmental Protection Agency, the EPA,

00:08:37.540 --> 00:08:41.820
looking at the exact same chemical and deeming it unlikely to cause cancer.

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:46.700
The EPA specifically does not require cancer warning labels on roundup bottles.

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:50.860
Help me understand this because on the surface, a skeptical consumer might just assume the

00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:53.700
EPA is bending to corporate lobbying.

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:59.060
Why do these two massive, incredibly well-funded global health authorities completely disagree?

00:08:59.060 --> 00:09:03.420
It is a brilliant question and it really comes down to the fundamental difference in how they

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:05.900
conduct their scientific assessments.

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:10.100
The World Health Organization conducts what is known as a hazard assessment.

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:15.820
They're essentially asking, can this chemical cause cancer in a vacuum at any theoretically

00:09:15.820 --> 00:09:16.820
possible dose?

00:09:16.820 --> 00:09:21.620
So like if you were exposed to massive concentrated amounts of it in a laboratory setting?

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:22.620
Yes.

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:27.620
For context, the WHO also classifies things like aloe vera extract, pickled vegetables,

00:09:27.620 --> 00:09:30.620
and working a night shift in similar hazard categories.

00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:31.620
Wow, okay.

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:34.060
The EPA, conversely, conducts a risk assessment.

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:38.540
They ask, will this chemical cause cancer at the normal exposure levels a human would

00:09:38.540 --> 00:09:40.780
realistically encounter?

00:09:40.780 --> 00:09:45.020
Like a home gardener spraying weeds in their driveway on a Saturday afternoon.

00:09:45.020 --> 00:09:47.260
I see the distinction now.

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:53.020
Hazard is about theoretical potential, but risk is about practical real-world application.

00:09:53.020 --> 00:09:58.460
And that profound regulatory discrepancy is the entire crux of the legal battle.

00:09:58.460 --> 00:10:03.100
Bayer has been completely mired in litigation over Roundup ever since they acquired Monsanto

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:04.100
back in 2018.

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:05.100
It's been everywhere.

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:10.540
We are talking about tens of thousands of lawsuits from everyday people, gardeners,

00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:16.340
groundskeepers, farmers claiming that long-term exposure to Roundup gave them cancer and suing

00:10:16.340 --> 00:10:19.540
because the company failed to put a warning label on the product.

00:10:19.540 --> 00:10:22.100
Which brings us to the Supreme Court's desk.

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:27.140
How does the highest court in the land resolve a situation where the Global Health Authority

00:10:27.140 --> 00:10:32.860
and the U.S. Environmental Authority are operating on two totally different scientific paradigms?

00:10:32.860 --> 00:10:37.340
Justice Brett Kavanaugh wrote the majority opinion and the ruling hinges entirely on a legal

00:10:37.340 --> 00:10:39.780
mechanism called federal preemption.

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:44.500
In short, the Supreme Court ruled that federal law strictly bars individual states from imposing

00:10:44.500 --> 00:10:47.940
different pesticide label requirements than the EPA.

00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:52.460
Meaning that because the EPA, the overarching federal authority says no warning label is needed,

00:10:52.460 --> 00:10:57.460
a state legislature or a state court in Missouri or California cannot turn around and penalize

00:10:57.460 --> 00:10:59.820
the company for not having a warning label.

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:01.220
That is the core logic.

00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:05.140
The court decided that if individual states were allowed to mandate their own health warnings

00:11:05.140 --> 00:11:10.020
based on local state level court decisions, a multinational company like Bayer would face

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:15.140
an unworkable patchwork of 50 different conflicting labeling requirements.

00:11:15.140 --> 00:11:19.140
So the federal EPA standard is now established as the absolute ceiling.

00:11:19.140 --> 00:11:22.740
State level failure to warn claims are officially preempted.

00:11:22.740 --> 00:11:25.980
The immediate real world fallout of this is immense.

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:32.700
For one, this specific ruling overturned a $1.25 million award from a Missouri jury to a gardener

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:35.900
who claimed his cancer originated from roundup exposure.

00:11:35.900 --> 00:11:40.340
It wipes that judgment out entirely and far more consequently for Bayer's bottom line,

00:11:40.340 --> 00:11:45.620
this decision essentially clears the legal runway for a massive $7.25 billion proposed

00:11:45.620 --> 00:11:50.300
class action settlement that will largely close the book on their entire roundup crisis.

00:11:50.300 --> 00:11:54.380
We should also highlight the political context here because it is incredibly tangled and create

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:59.580
some very strange bedfellows and to be absolutely clear to everyone listening.

00:11:59.580 --> 00:12:03.780
We are not taking aside on the underlying science of glyphosate or the legal merits

00:12:03.780 --> 00:12:05.580
of the Supreme Court's ruling.

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:09.500
We are simply reporting the fascinating political alliances this case has forged.

00:12:09.500 --> 00:12:12.820
It genuinely scrambles the traditional partisan lines.

00:12:12.820 --> 00:12:18.340
On one hand, this Supreme Court decision is undeniably a massive win for the global pesticide

00:12:18.340 --> 00:12:19.340
industry.

00:12:19.340 --> 00:12:24.420
It is also a major validated victory for the former Trump administration, which actually

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:29.340
filed briefs urging the Supreme Court to side with Bayer in this exact dispute.

00:12:29.340 --> 00:12:34.060
But on the flip side, this ruling has absolutely infuriated the followers of Robert F. Kennedy

00:12:34.060 --> 00:12:35.060
Jr.

00:12:35.060 --> 00:12:41.420
That particular political camp is staunchly aggressively opposed to glyphosate and agricultural chemicals

00:12:41.420 --> 00:12:45.900
and they are incredibly vocal about their displeasure with this corporate friendly Trump

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:47.140
supported decision.

00:12:47.140 --> 00:12:52.300
It is a perfect illustration of how messy things get when complex, highly debated scientific

00:12:52.300 --> 00:12:57.220
uncertainty is forced into a rigid binary legal framework.

00:12:57.220 --> 00:13:01.180
The legal system had to make a black and white choice, can state sue or not, even though

00:13:01.180 --> 00:13:06.620
the scientific consensus regarding hazard versus risk is still full of gray areas globally.

00:13:06.620 --> 00:13:11.940
Science is outpacing the courts, artificial intelligence is outpacing the Federal Reserve.

00:13:11.940 --> 00:13:16.260
And if we look at our final source today, we see that modern financial reality is completely

00:13:16.260 --> 00:13:18.820
outpacing the rule book in college sports.

00:13:18.820 --> 00:13:24.900
Yes, we are looking at the NCAA, the governing body of collegiate athletics, which has officially

00:13:24.980 --> 00:13:29.780
announced a sweeping regulatory shift that is immediately resulting in lawsuits and administrative

00:13:29.780 --> 00:13:30.780
chaos.

00:13:30.780 --> 00:13:31.780
Walk us through the new rule.

00:13:31.780 --> 00:13:34.620
What exactly is the NCAA trying to change?

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:39.660
They are implementing a strict, hard-capped, five-year eligibility limit for student athletes.

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:44.780
This new policy goes into full effect, starting in the 2027-2028 academic year.

00:13:44.780 --> 00:13:49.380
The eligibility clock will start ticking the moment a student enrolls full time or the academic

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:51.780
year after they turn 19, whichever comes first.

00:13:51.780 --> 00:13:57.260
Which means an athlete gets a five-year window to play four seasons of their sport,

00:13:57.260 --> 00:13:59.820
and then their time is unequivocally up.

00:13:59.820 --> 00:14:05.420
There are only extremely rare exceptions allowed for, like pregnancy or military service.

00:14:05.420 --> 00:14:09.860
The NCAA designed this rule to firmly put an end to the current phenomenon where some

